<languages />
<translate>
<!--T:1-->
Configuration lives in a <code>.wikven.yaml</code> file in your source directory. It follows the same shape as [<tvar name="1">https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Special:MyLanguage/Manual:YAML_settings_file_format</tvar> MediaWiki's own YAML settings format]: a top-level <code>extensions</code> list, a <code>skins</code> list, and a <code>config</code> map.

<!--T:2-->
The file name is flexible. Wikven accepts <code>.wikven.yaml</code>, <code>.wikven.yml</code> or <code>.wikven.json</code>, and the same three without the leading dot (<code>wikven.yaml</code>, <code>wikven.yml</code>, <code>wikven.json</code>); the <code>.json</code> forms use the same structure, since YAML is a superset of JSON. If more than one is present the first wins, in that order, and the rest are ignored with a warning, so a dotted name beats a plain one, YAML beats JSON, and <code>.yaml</code> beats <code>.yml</code>.

<!--T:3-->
Wikven ships its own defaults (a sensible MediaWiki configuration for a static wiki) and loads your file on top of them, so anything you set here overrides the default. The defaults live in a <code>default.yml</code> that uses this same format; see [[#Defaults]] below.
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="yaml" copy>
extensions:
  - SyntaxHighlight_GeSHi
  - ParserFunctions
skins:
  - MinervaNeue
config:
  Sitename: Wikven
  WikvenEditUrl: https://github.com/yourname/some-repository/edit/branch-name/path/to/$1
  WikvenHistoryUrl: https://github.com/yourname/some-repository/commits/branch-name/path/to/$1
</syntaxhighlight>
<translate>
<!--T:4-->
== <code>extensions</code> ==
A list of extension names to load. An extension that ships inside {{SITENAME}} works by name alone; any other name is treated as third-party and fetched from a source you declare in the [[#WikvenRepositories|<code>WikvenRepositories</code> map]]. See [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Extensions</tvar>|Extensions]] for what ships and what does not.

<!--T:5-->
== <code>skins</code> ==
A list of skin names to enable. The first one is used as the default skin. As with extensions, a non-bundled name is fetched from the [[#WikvenRepositories|<code>WikvenRepositories</code> map]]. See [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Skins</tvar>|Skins]].

<!--T:6-->
== <code>config</code> ==

<!--T:7-->
A map of configuration variables, each named without the <code>wg</code> prefix, just like in MediaWiki's YAML settings format. Any [<tvar name="1">https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Special:MyLanguage/Manual:Configuration_settings</tvar> configuration setting] can be defined here, and so can Wikven's own variables, which the sections below describe one by one.

<!--T:36-->
Three more exist and are not for you to set: <code>WikvenSourceDirectory</code>, <code>WikvenHtmlDirectory</code> and <code>WikvenSourceHistoryFile</code> are derived from <code>WIKVEN_WORKDIR</code> by the build itself (see [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Commands</tvar>|Commands]]). Setting them in your file does nothing useful, and the build overwrites them anyway.

<!--T:8-->
=== <code>WikvenLogos</code> ===

<!--T:45-->
'''Default:''' empty

<!--T:9-->
The site's header logos. It mirrors MediaWiki's [<tvar name="1">https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Special:MyLanguage/Manual:$wgLogos</tvar> $wgLogos], with the same keys (<code>icon</code>, <code>1x</code>, <code>svg</code>, <code>wordmark</code>, and so on), except each value is the name of an image file in your source directory rather than a URL:
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="yaml" copy>
config:
  WikvenLogos:
    icon: logo.svg
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:10-->
The values are file names rather than URLs because the file's address is decided by the build, not by you. Each named file is uploaded into the <code>File:</code> namespace like any other source image, and Wikven points <code>$wgLogos</code> at the upload for you, so you only name the file. The export then copies the logo to a single shared file next to the HTML and rewrites the reference to it, the same as for any other image, so the logo is not embedded into every page. Name none and the skin keeps whatever logo MediaWiki itself would show.

<!--T:11-->
Wikven also ships a small default favicon so browsers do not 404 on <code>/favicon.ico</code>; override it with <code>config.Favicon</code> (a URL or a data URI).

<!--T:12-->
<span id="WikvenEditUrl"></span>
=== <code>WikvenEditUrl</code>, <code>WikvenHistoryUrl</code> and <code>WikvenViewSourceUrl</code> ===

<!--T:46-->
'''Default:''' empty

<!--T:13-->
The targets the "Edit", "View history" and "View source" links point at, so a reader can jump from a rendered page to its source in your repository. In each URL, <code>$1</code> is replaced with the page's source file name relative to your source directory, including its extension. A page whose title carries its own content model keeps that name verbatim (<code>MediaWiki:Common.css</code>, <code>MediaWiki:Common.js</code>, a <code>.json</code> config page, a <code>Module:</code> page, ...); every other page is a <code>.wikitext</code> file, so a normal page becomes <code>Getting Started.wikitext</code>. Leave any of them unset to drop that link; a generated page (such as the [[#WikvenAboutPage|About]] page, when the build writes it) has no source file, so these links are omitted for it.

<!--T:14-->
=== <code>WikvenMainPage</code> ===

<!--T:47-->
'''Default:''' <code>index</code>

<!--T:15-->
The title of the page used as the site root, written as <code>index.html</code> and served at the top of the site. Naming your entry file <code>index.wikitext</code> therefore takes no configuration at all. The page must be one you imported; the build fails if it is missing.

<!--T:16-->
=== <code>WikvenAboutPage</code> ===

<!--T:48-->
'''Default:''' <code>About</code>

<!--T:17-->
The title of the page that introduces your site, which MediaWiki's own "About" footer link is pointed at. Write a source page of that name and it is used exactly as written. With no source page of that name, the build writes one holding an introduction with the software list under it. Set it empty to write no page at all, and the footer link goes with it.

<!--T:55-->
Whatever this is set to, the build generates two templates, since an export has no <code>Special:Version</code> to send a reader to. Transclude either from any page you like: <code><nowiki>{{Wikven software}}</nowiki></code> is what that page would show — MediaWiki, PHP and the database, then the extensions and skins — and <code><nowiki>{{Wikven components}}</nowiki></code> is the extensions and skins alone, with the license each one declares, which is what a page acknowledging what your site redistributes wants.

<!--T:18-->
=== <code>WikvenFooterUrl</code> ===

<!--T:49-->
'''Default:''' empty

<!--T:19-->
A link added to the site footer, pointing at your project (typically its repository). The footer shows the host name for known forges (GitHub, GitLab, Codeberg, ...). With no URL there is no such link.

<!--T:37-->
=== <code>WikvenSettingsPage</code> ===

<!--T:50-->
'''Default:''' <code>Settings</code>

<!--T:38-->
The title of a page the build writes for the reader's own display choices: the colour theme, and, on a site built with more than one skin, which skin to read it in. The choices are kept in the reader's browser and apply to that site alone; no account is involved. It stands in for the preferences a live wiki would offer, which a static export has no server for.

<!--T:39-->
Write a source page of that name and it takes precedence, exactly as for the About page. Set it empty to write no page at all, and the entries pointing at it go with it. See [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Skins#settings</tvar>|Skins]] for what the page offers a reader.

<!--T:40-->
=== <code>WikvenBundleWebfonts</code> ===

<!--T:51-->
'''Default:''' <code>false</code>

<!--T:41-->
When set, and UniversalLanguageSelector is enabled, the build bakes that extension's webfonts for the languages your pages use into a stylesheet the export serves itself, so a reader whose system has no font for a script sees the intended typeface instead of empty boxes. It is off unless asked for, because the fonts are extra payload for a site whose readers may not need them. It does nothing without UniversalLanguageSelector; see [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Translating</tvar>|Translating]].

<!--T:56-->
=== <code>WikvenSkinPreview</code> ===

<!--T:57-->
'''Default:''' <code>false</code>

<!--T:58-->
For looking at a skin rather than publishing a site. {{SITENAME}} normally trims the chrome around a page to what a static host can stand behind: the personal menu, the toolbox, the talk tab and the watch star are emptied, the article tabs are replaced with links to your source files, a link to the repository goes in the footer, and a stylesheet of its own hides the rest. Every one of those is a judgement about a reader, and none of them is wanted by a skin author who has pointed the build at a few pages to see how their skin renders: the menus being edited are the thing they came to look at. Set this and they are all left as the skin drew them.

<!--T:59-->
What the build still does is everything that makes the output a working set of files. Links are rewritten to <code>./Page.html</code>, styles and scripts are baked into local copies, and the two fixes that stop a page asking for something no export has -- Citizen's service worker and its search shortcuts -- stay on, because a preview has no <code>load.php</code> behind it either. The generated [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Configuration#WikvenAboutPage</tvar>|About]] and [[<tvar name="2">Special:MyLanguage/Configuration#WikvenSettingsPage</tvar>|Settings]] pages are content rather than chrome and are still written; set those empty to leave them out too.

<!--T:60-->
Leave it off for a site anyone reads. A published export that offers a login, a watchlist and a talk tab with nothing behind any of them is worse than one that offers none.

<!--T:61-->
There is a source tree to bake with it: <code>examples/skin-preview</code> in the {{SITENAME}} repository, one page carrying every element a skin has to style. Add your skin to its <code>.wikven.yml</code> and build that directory.

<!--T:42-->
<span id="WikvenStyleDirectory"></span>
=== <code>WikvenStyleDirectory</code> and <code>WikvenScriptDirectory</code> ===

<!--T:52-->
'''Default:''' <code>.</code>

<!--T:43-->
Where under the output directory the build writes the CSS and JavaScript it dumps out of MediaWiki. <code>.</code> is the output root. Set them to keep the generated assets out of the way of your pages; the paths are relative to the output directory and the build writes the links to match, so nothing else needs telling.
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="yaml" copy>
config:
  WikvenStyleDirectory: assets
  WikvenScriptDirectory: assets
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:20-->
=== <code>WikvenRepositories</code> ===

<!--T:53-->
'''Default:''' empty

<!--T:21-->
Sources for the third-party extensions and skins you list in <code>extensions</code> and <code>skins</code>. The lists stay plain names (the shape MediaWiki itself uses); this map says where to get the ones that are not bundled with wikven. They are fetched at build time, before MediaWiki loads them. Whether an entry is an extension or a skin is taken from which list its name appears in, so you never write the name twice. A name listed in <code>extensions</code> or <code>skins</code> that is neither bundled nor declared here is skipped with a warning.

<!--T:22-->
Each entry chooses one of three methods by the key it carries:
</translate>

<translate nowrap>
<!--T:34-->
<syntaxhighlight lang="yaml" copy>
<tvar name="1">config:
  WikvenRepositories:</tvar>
    # 1. A tarball, e.g. from Special:ExtensionDistributor, whose archive already
    #    bundles the extension's dependencies. Add sha256 to verify the download.
    <tvar name="2">Foo:
      tarball: https://extdist.wmflabs.org/dist/extensions/Foo-REL1_46-abc1234.tar.gz
      sha256: 4f5e...</tvar>  # 64 hex characters; the build aborts on a mismatch
    # 2. A Git repository. Pin it with commit (an exact SHA, reproducible) or
    #    reference (a tag or branch). Add composer: true to run Composer inside
    #    the cloned directory.
    <tvar name="3">Bar:
      repository: https://github.com/example/Bar.git
      commit: 1a2b3c4d5e6f7a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b
      composer: true</tvar>
    # 3. A Composer package, resolved through MediaWiki's composer.local.json.
    <tvar name="4">SemanticMediaWiki:
      package: mediawiki/semantic-media-wiki:~4.1</tvar>
</syntaxhighlight>
</translate>

<translate>
<!--T:23-->
A name in <code>WikvenRepositories</code> that is already bundled with wikven is left untouched. A name that is not also listed in <code>extensions</code> or <code>skins</code> is an error.

<!--T:24-->
Each method needs a host tool present at build time: <code>tarball</code> uses <code>curl</code> and <code>tar</code>, <code>repository</code> uses <code>git</code> (and [<tvar name="2">https://getcomposer.org/</tvar> Composer] when <code>composer: true</code>), and <code>package</code> uses Composer. The Docker image bundles all of these; with the standalone [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Standalone binary</tvar>|binary]] they must be on your <code>PATH</code>.

<!--T:25-->
{{note|The build downloads and '''runs''' the code you name here, with network access: a cloned extension's PHP and any Composer scripts execute during the build. Only declare sources you trust. For a tamper-evident, reproducible build, pin a tarball with <code>sha256</code> and a repository with <code>commit</code> (an exact SHA); a mutable <code>reference</code> tag/branch or a floating <code>package</code> constraint can change underneath you.}}

<!--T:44-->
Nothing moves an exact pin for you, though: this file belongs to no ecosystem a dependency bot reads, so a pin left alone is a pin left behind. See [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Deploying#keeping-pins-current</tvar>|Deploying]] for keeping one current.

<!--T:26-->
This very site fetches [<tvar name="2">https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Special:MyLanguage/Extension:TabberNeue</tvar> TabberNeue] this way (see its <code>.wikven.yaml</code>): the tabbed Docker/binary command examples on [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Getting Started</tvar>|Getting Started]] are rendered by the fetched extension. (TemplateStyles, used by this site's templates, is bundled in MediaWiki and so needs no source here.)

<!--T:27-->
=== <code>ContentNamespaces</code> ===

<!--T:54-->
'''Default:''' <code>[0, 6]</code>

<!--T:28-->
A standard MediaWiki setting ([<tvar name="2">https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Special:MyLanguage/Manual:$wgContentNamespaces</tvar> <code>$wgContentNamespaces</code>]) that decides which pages wikven exports: only pages in a content namespace are written to the static site. Wikven sets the main and File: namespaces, so file description pages are exported without dumping every <code>Template:</code> or <code>MediaWiki:</code> page. Add the Category namespace (<code>14</code>) to export category pages too; see [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Pages#categories</tvar>|Pages#Categories]]. Because a <code>config</code> value replaces the default rather than extending it, list the full set you want, including the defaults you keep.

<!--T:29-->
== Defaults ==

<!--T:30-->
Before reading your <code>.wikven.yaml</code>, Wikven applies its own defaults from a <code>default.yml</code> bundled with wikven. It is written in the same format as your configuration, so it doubles as a reference for what Wikven sets for you: the site name, page titles kept as written, InstantCommons, local image uploads (including SVG), the Vector 2022 skin options, and so on. It also ships an <code>extensions</code> and a <code>skins</code> default, so every site gets [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Searching</tvar>|SifterSearch]] search and the Vector skin without listing them. Your <code>.wikven.yaml</code> is merged on top, with your <code>config</code> keys overriding the defaults and your <code>extensions</code> and <code>skins</code> appended.

<!--T:31-->
Titles are kept as written (rather than first-letter-capitalized, MediaWiki's default) so the entry page can be the lowercase <code>index</code>, which the build writes as <code>index.html</code>, the file a static host serves at the site root. The file is otherwise commented; each comment notes why Wikven sets that value.

<!--T:32-->
To see the current defaults, read the bundled <code>default.yml</code>. From the Docker image:
</translate>

<syntaxhighlight lang="shell" copy>
docker run --rm --entrypoint cat ghcr.io/chaotic-ground/wikven extensions/Wikven/default.yml
</syntaxhighlight>

<translate>
<!--T:33-->
It is also in the [https://github.com/chaotic-ground/wikven/blob/main/default.yml repository].
</translate>

